        &hellip;such as the well known Kolmogorov-Smirnov or Anderson-Darling tests, in particular when the number of samples is small and differences occur over a small quantile of the null <span class="search-hit mathjax">hypothesis</span> distribution. A detailed description of the test statistic is provided including a detailed discussion of the parameterization of its distribution via asymptotic bootstrappin&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2111.02252v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2111.02252v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2111.02252v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        We introduce a new statistical test based on the observed spacings of ordered data. The statistic is sensitive to detect non-uniformity in random samples, or short-lived features in event time series. Under some conditions, this new test can outperform existing ones, such as the well known Kolmogorov-Smirnov or Anderson-Darling tests, in particular when the number of samples is small and differences occur over a small quantile of the null <span class="search-hit mathjax">hypothesis</span> distribution. A detailed description of the test statistic is provided including a detailed discussion of the parameterization of its distribution via asymptotic bootstrapping as well as a novel per-quantile error estimation of the empirical distribution. Two example applications are provided, using the test to boost the sensitivity in generic &#34;bump <span class="search-hit mathjax">hunting</span>&#34;, and employing the test to detect supernovae. The article is rounded off with an extended performance comparison to other, established goodness-of-fit tests.
